1.text:
For an air filled ionisation chamber the charge which is collected per unit
time is

* $P_{\rm abs}$ energy absorbed per unit time in the air which is in the
beam

\begin{equation*}
P_{\rm abs}= \frac{I}{e}\,{W}= I\frac{W}{e}\;,
\qquad\frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
so that the air kerma rate in the volume which is irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{P_{\rm abs}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I}{m_{a}}\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}
in the usual  $g\approx 0$  approximation.  With the beam at the focus
$A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}$ and a
depth of air of $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}A_{f}L
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
      \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\,{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is actually in the beam.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
P_{\rm abs}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J/C}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the free-in-air kerma is the surface kerma which
would be produced in tissue in the same fluence.

A material point which is moved with the scan spends in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t =\frac{w_{f}}{v}
         =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{\rm exp}}
\end{equation*}
in the beam.  In the notation of the statement

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t = \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{\rm exp}} =0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
is the dwell time of a point in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot.
The kerma received by a point in one passage through the monochromatic
spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,\Delta t
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu {\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$
exposure at $33\;{\rm keV}$ in the $0.30\ {\rm mm}\times6.0\ {\rm mm}$ spot.
This is the usual “per–projection’’ surface kerma which is quoted for the
synchrotron micro-focus system, $ \dot K_{a}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus.

In particular the $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal footprint on the Laue
monochromator is imaged with a demagnification
$M_{A}=A_{m}/A_{f}=50\times6/(0.30\times6)\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$ onto the
$0.30\,{\rm mm}$ spot.  Integrated over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}$
energy-spread which can be stepped through with the scan the
energy-integrated surface kerma is

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm int}\simeq K_{1}\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}
          \simeq K_{1}\times\frac{50}{0.30}
          \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ for the complete $ \approx 167$
energy positions covered by the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
illuminated footprint, $ \approx 0.65\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted
above, which is the often quoted cumulative multiple–energy surface dose
for a full scan of the Laue monochromator field of view.  The per-point,
per-energy dose quoted above is the $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$
passage normally quoted for the system.

