1.text:
The free in air air kerma in the micro spot can be obtained from the
ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=I\frac{W}{e} \;, \qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.9\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is actually in the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm gcm^{-3}}\times0.272\;{\rm 
cm^{3}}\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
      \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{P_{abs}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
       \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
the free-in-air kerma rate is taken as the soft tissue free-in-air
surface kerma rate at the focus.

A material point spends

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t =0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

in the monochromatic $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ spot on a single pass.  The
monochromatic, per-pass surface kerma is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}=\dot K_{a}\,\Delta t\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx4\times10^{-3}\;{\rm mGy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per
$0.02\;{\rm s}$ $33\;{\rm keV}$ passage in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times
6.0\,{\rm mm}$ microbeam.  With the conventional $W_{air}=33.7\;{\rm
J\,C^{-1}}$ $K_{1}\simeq3.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic projection,
the familiar synchrotron micro-beam surface dose.  This is the dose
received by a point for one energy.

The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the Laue crystal is
demagnified on the focus by

\begin{equation*}
M_{A}\simeq\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\approx167
\end{equation*}

essentially independent monochromatic positions/energy steps.  The
total scan time that covers the whole $W_{m}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ Laue
field is $t_{scan}=M_{A}\Delta t$.  The peak energy-integrated free-in-air
surface kerma for a point which receives the whole $W_{m}/w_{f}$ set of
passes is

\begin{equation*}
K_{int}\simeq N K_{1}\simeq K_{1}\frac{W_{m}}{w_{f}}
      \simeq3.8\times10^{-6}\times167\;{\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

$\simeq0.65\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted, $\simeq0.65\;{\rm mrad}$
free-in-air, essentially the same in soft tissue for
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$.
In terms of the area average over the $W_{m}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ raster the
average surface kerma remains the $K_{1}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.004\;{\rm mGy}$ per energy pass, $\dot K_{a}w_{f}/W_{m}$ averaged over
the raster, i.e. $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the focus.  The
$\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ value is the commonly quoted cumulative
peak multiple-energy surface kerma for a complete scan of the 50 mm Laue
field, $167$ times the $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ monochromatic per-projection
dose.  This is the $\sim0.65\;{\rm mGy}$ total deposited energy
$P_{abs}t_{scan}$ spread over the raster. ∎

